@meta
id: 17-19
title: Capstone — getting back where you were
chapter: 17
type: scenario
skills: jump-back, jumplist, marks-set, marks-jump, match-percent
par: 90
minutes: 6
vscode: Alt+Left, F12, Ctrl+Shift+Backslash
open: order.provider.ts answers.txt
vimrc: set number

@brief
Every jump you make - a search, a G, a % - is recorded. Ctrl-O walks
back through that history and Ctrl-I walks forward again. It is
Alt+Left / Alt+Right, and it works across files.

This is the fix for "I jumped to look at something and now I have
lost my place".

In order.provider.ts, answer the three bracket questions.

Then, for practice that nobody is grading: put the cursor somewhere
in the middle, press ma to drop a mark called a, press G to go to
the bottom, then 'a to come back. Do it a few times. Marks and
Ctrl-O are what let you roam a file fearlessly.

@keys
%            jump to the matching bracket  ( ) [ ] { }
Ctrl-O       go back to where I just was      (works across files)
Ctrl-I       go forward again
``           back to the exact spot before the last big jump
''           back to the LINE before the last big jump
ma           drop a mark named a here     'a  jump back to it
'A           marks with capital letters work across files
gd           go to the local definition of the word under the cursor
gf           open the file whose name is under the cursor
Ctrl-^       flip between this file and the one before it
:jumps       show the jump history
zz  zt  zb   redraw with the current line centred / at top / bottom

@hints
:set number , then 8G to land on line 8, then f( to get onto the
bracket, then % .
--
% only works when the cursor is actually on a bracket. If nothing
happens, you are on the wrong character - use f( or f{ to land on it.
--
After each % you can press Ctrl-O to come straight back to the line
you started on, ready for the next question.

@solution
:set number
8G f( %     read the line number   Ctrl-O
7G f{ %     read it                Ctrl-O
16G f{ %    read it

:bn , fill in answers.txt, :wa

The one to actually internalise is Ctrl-O. Once you trust it you
will search across the whole repo just to glance at something,
because getting back is one keystroke.

@start answers.txt
# % jumps between a bracket and its partner. Put the cursor on a
# bracket and press % . Line numbers are already on.

line_matching_the_open_paren_on_line_8=
line_matching_the_open_brace_on_line_7=
line_matching_the_open_brace_on_line_16=

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected answers.txt
